xmlns:fr="http://www.jonmsterling.com/jms-005P.xml"><fr:frontmatter><fr:anchor>620</fr:anchor><fr:addr>hejohns-000L</fr:addr><fr:route>hejohns-000L.xml</fr:route><fr:title>Generalized element</fr:title><fr:taxon>Definition</fr:taxon><fr:date><fr:year>2024</fr:year><fr:month>6</fr:month><fr:day>3</fr:day></fr:date><fr:date><fr:year>2024</fr:year><fr:month>6</fr:month><fr:day>4</fr:day></fr:date><fr:authors /></fr:frontmatter><fr:mainmatter><fr:p>
    A <fr:em>generalized element</fr:em> (alt. local element) of an object <fr:tex
display="inline"><![CDATA[A ∈     \mathcal {C}]]></fr:tex> is <fr:em>just</fr:em> a morphism <fr:tex
display="inline"><![CDATA[e : B → A]]></fr:tex>, for some <fr:tex
display="inline"><![CDATA[B]]></fr:tex>. Ie, a
    generalized element of <fr:tex
display="inline"><![CDATA[A]]></fr:tex> is <fr:em>just</fr:em> an object of the slice over <fr:tex
display="inline"><![CDATA[A]]></fr:tex>.
</fr:p><fr:p>
    There's supposed to be some Kripke-style interpretation of these as
    elements defined at points in time, but TODO.
</fr:p><fr:p>
    From <fr:link
type="local"
href="mclarty-1992.xml"
addr="mclarty-1992"
title="Elementary Categories, Elementary Toposes">Elementary Categories, Elementary Toposes</fr:link>. Compare with <fr:link
type="local"
href="hejohns-000K.xml"
addr="hejohns-000K"
title="Global element">global element</fr:link>.
</fr:p></fr:mainmatter><fr:backmatter /></fr:tree><fr:tree
toc="true"
